{"abstract":"Much work in HCI has investigated strategies for supporting au-tonomous self-regulation in social media use (SMU): helping usersto control their time online and ensure it serves personally valuedoutcomes. However, results suggest that the effectiveness and ac-ceptability of these strategies may vary based on individual needs.Recent work has attributed this variation to motivational factors,though we currently lack data to understand how these factorsinfluence self-regulation, user experience and well-being. We drawon Self-Determination Theory to analyse autonomous and non-autonomous patterns of motivation in 521 users of social media.Using latent profile analysis, we identify 4 “motivational profiles”associated with significant differences in need satisfaction, affect,and compulsive engagement. Our results clarify distinct aspectsof autonomy in SMU and identify opportunities to target and per-sonalise design interventions; they suggest autonomous regulationcan be associated with better experience and well-being, thoughnot necessarily less time online.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bennett, Daniel","Feng, Feng","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-861030"],"event":{"end":"2021-05-13","place":"Yokohama, jp","start":"2021-05-08","title":"CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2026-07","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Autonomous motivation","Self determination theory","Social media use","Well being"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"autonomous regulation of social media use implications for self control well being and ux","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713094","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funded by the European Union (ERC, THEORYCRAFT, 101043198). Views and opinions expressed are however those of the authors only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union or the European Research Council Executive Agency. Neither the European Union nor the granting authority can be held responsible for them."],"id":"S01-000.00-861030","identifier":[{"id":"105005749039","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8076c010-688a-49a8-ad10-e9881a62aa1e"],"keyword":["Self-Determination Theory","autonomous motivation","personalized design interventions","social media use","well-being"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-0EBE2E6B7AD4ADF460C51C8272C18CF1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/58jgc_v1","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713094","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel Thomas","key":"bennettpau","last":"Bennett","locid":"ca2a035e-64a1-4a75-9269-d6ee91496191","name":"Bennett, Daniel","name_org":"Bennett, Daniel Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Feng","key":"fengpau","last":"Feng","locid":"dac0e506-da5d-4ee6-8783-5abd2a2096f3","name":"Feng, Feng","orcid":"0000-0002-7127-5437","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa D","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","name_org":"Mekler, Elisa D","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31234/osf.io/58jgc_v1","isbn":["979840071394"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"ACM Annual Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems","status":"p","title":"CHI '25: Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing System"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8076c010-688a-49a8-ad10-e9881a62aa1e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Autonomous Regulation of Social Media Use: Implications for Self-control, Well-Being, and UX","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-861030","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"A “warm” smile? A “cold” rage? Thermal and emotional experiences are often tightly connected. In recent years HCI has investigated opportunities for thermal technologies to enrich user experience. However, our vocabulary for these experiences has remained limited and inexpressive: “hot”, “cold”, “warm”, “cool”. This limits our ability to describe and conceptualise experience, and impedes the exploration of design features for thermal and affective interaction. Our pictorial offers resources to enrich language and clarify the design space for thermal and affective technologies. We expand a recently developed theoretical framework based on Chinese classical poetry to present six lenses on thermal and affective experience. The pictorial also functions as a deck of design cards with a design exemplar. Together these resources can help researchers and designers conceptualise thermal experience, explore parameters for experiential design, and design technologies for rich thermal and affective experience.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Feng, Feng","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S02-081.90-835208"],"event":{"acronym":"TEI","end":"2025-03-07","place":"Colorado , fr","start":"2025-03-04","title":"Conference on Tangible, Embedded, and Embodied Interaction","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2026-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bristol"],"keyword":["Affect","Affect emotion","Chinese poetry","Cross culture","Design cards","Design principle","Emotion","Experiential quality","Thermal interaction"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"thermal cards from classical chinese poetry to designerly len